This version works directly with the two ends of each substring. For a state `(left, right)`, matching characters let us move inward without adding anything. If they differ, one insertion is needed, and the code tries both possible sides.

The memo table is important because the same ranges appear in many recursive calls. A string with zero or one character already is a palindrome, so the answer is zero for those cases.

```java
class Solution {
    private int[][] memo;

    private int solve(String s, int left, int right) {
        if (left >= right) {
            return 0;
        }

        if (memo[left][right] != -1) {
            return memo[left][right];
        }

        if (s.charAt(left) == s.charAt(right)) {
            memo[left][right] = solve(s, left + 1, right - 1);
        } else {
            int skipLeft = solve(s, left + 1, right);
            int skipRight = solve(s, left, right - 1);
            memo[left][right] = 1 + Math.min(skipLeft, skipRight);
        }

        return memo[left][right];
    }

    public int minInsertion(String s) {
        int n = s.length();
        if (n < 2) {
            return 0;
        }

        memo = new int[n][n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            java.util.Arrays.fill(memo[i], -1);
        }

        return solve(s, 0, n - 1);
    }
}
```

The running time is `O(n^2)`, since there are at most that many substring ranges. Space usage is `O(n^2)` for `memo`, plus `O(n)` for the recursion stack.

This is another way to use the LPS observation. The original string and its reverse are compared as an LCS problem, but there is no need to keep every row of the table.

`previous` stores the row before the current one, and `current` stores the row being filled. After each row, the two vectors are swapped. This keeps the memory small while using the same LCS transitions.

One edge case is an already palindromic string such as `"racecar"`. Its LPS has the full length, so the returned value is zero.

```cpp
class Solution{   
public: int minInsertion(string s) {
        int n = s.size();
        if (n < 2) {
            return 0;
        }

        string reversed = s;
        reverse(reversed.begin(), reversed.end());

        vector<int> previous(n + 1, 0);
        vector<int> current(n + 1, 0);

        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            fill(current.begin(), current.end(), 0);

            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                if (s[i - 1] == reversed[j - 1]) {
                    current[j] = previous[j - 1] + 1;
                } else {
                    current[j] = max(previous[j], current[j - 1]);
                }
            }

            swap(previous, current);
        }

        return n - previous[n];
  }
};
```

The nested loops give `O(n^2)` time. The two vectors use `O(n)` space, and the input string is left unchanged.

I used an interval DP table here. `dp[i][j]` stores the minimum insertions needed for the part of the string from `i` to `j`.

When both ends match, they need no new character. When they do not match, either the left character or the right character can be matched, so the smaller result is chosen.

For example, with `s = "mbadm"`, the outer `m` characters match. The inside range is then solved, and the final answer is `2`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int minInsertion(string s) {
        int n = s.size();
        if (n < 2) {
            return 0;
        }

        vector<vector<int>> dp(n, vector<int>(n, 0));

        for (int length = 2; length <= n; length++) {
            for (int left = 0; left + length <= n; left++) {
                int right = left + length - 1;

                if (s[left] == s[right]) {
                    dp[left][right] = (length == 2) ? 0 : dp[left + 1][right - 1];
                } else {
                    dp[left][right] = 1 + min(dp[left + 1][right], dp[left][right - 1]);
                }
            }
        }

        return dp[0][n - 1];
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.

Space complexity: `O(n^2)` for the table. The input string is not changed.

Instead of building the palindrome, this uses the longest common subsequence of the string and its reverse. That LCS length is the longest palindromic subsequence length.

If `n` is the string length and the LPS has length `same`, then only the other `n - same` characters need matching insertions. The full table makes the transition easy to inspect and debug.

For `"abcda"`, the LPS can be `"aca"` or `"ada"`, depending on the choices in the table. The answer is the string length minus that LPS length.

```java
class Solution {
    public int minInsertion(String s) {
        int n = s.length();
        if (n < 2) {
            return 0;
        }

        String reversed = new StringBuilder(s).reverse().toString();
        int[][] dp = new int[n + 1][n + 1];

        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                if (s.charAt(i - 1) == reversed.charAt(j - 1)) {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;
                } else {
                    dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
                }
            }
        }

        return n - dp[n][n];
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.

Space complexity: `O(n^2)`, because the complete LCS table is stored. The original string is not changed.
